  77: ## 2. The fix â€” derive the type from the producer; delete the ability to declare it
  78: 
  79: **Decision D1. The suite stops naming its types. It names its requirement and its sut key, and the
  80: types are read off the adapter that actually supplies them.**
  81: 
  82: A new types-only module, `tests/at/harness/suite-adapters.ts`, holds the one declaration:
  83: 
  84: ```ts
  85: export type AdapterModules = {
  86:   'req-016': typeof import('../suites/req-016/_fixture.ts');
  87: };
  88: ```
  89: 
  90: and the derivations:
  91: 
  92: ```ts
  93: export type SuiteId = keyof AdapterModules;
  94: type AdapterOf<R extends SuiteId> = Awaited<ReturnType<AdapterModules[R]['createFixtureAdapter']>>;
  95: export type SutMapOf<R extends SuiteId> = AdapterOf<R>['sut'];
  96: export type SutKeyOf<R extends SuiteId> = keyof SutMapOf<R> & string;
  97: export type SutOf<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>> = SutMapOf<R>[K];
  98: export type WorldOf<R extends SuiteId> = Awaited<ReturnType<AdapterOf<R>['fixtures']['world']>>;
  99: ```
 100: 
 101: `typeof import(...)` in a type position is erased at emit, so this adds **no runtime edge** from
 102: the harness to the suites. It mirrors, at compile time, the resolution
 103: `tests/at/harness/index.ts:32` already performs at run time.
 104: 
 105: `bindSuite` then becomes:
 106: 
 107: ```ts
 108: export function bindSuite<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>>(binding: {
 109:   requirement: R;
 110:   sut: K;
 111:   sutMissingDetail?: string;
 112: }): /* an atTest bound to SutOf<R, K> and WorldOf<R> */
 113: ```
 114: 
 115: **What this buys, concretely:**
 116: 
 117: 1. **There is no longer a type argument to lie with.** `bindSuite<NotificationsSut, World>` becomes
 118:    an arity error â€” the same defence, and the same diagnostic (TS2558), that killed the harness-type
 119:    attack in AI4DEV-24.
 120: 2. **The producer is the single source.** `_fixture.ts` already annotates `const sut: NotificationsSut`,
 121:    so the shape is checked where it is written. The consumer now *reads that* instead of restating it.
 122:    This is the brief's instruction â€” make the producer statically checked rather than asserting at the
 123:    consumer â€” satisfied without touching the factory.
 124: 3. **A mistyped sut key is a compile error.** `K extends SutKeyOf<R>` rejects `sut: 'notificatoins'`
 125:    where today it compiles and fails at run time as `sut-missing`.
 126: 4. **A required member the adapter does not supply now goes red at the adapter** (TS2741), because
 127:    there is only one type and the producer is annotated with it.
 128: 
 129: **Decision D2. `W` travels from the same adapter, but by its own path â€” it is not welded to `Sut`
 130: and it is never welded to the harness.**
 131: 
 132: `WorldOf<R>` derives from `fixtures.world`'s return type; `SutOf<R, K>` derives from `sut[K]`. Same
 133: source, separate derivations. The file warns twice that these were once conflated
 134: (`registry.ts:160-175`, `contracts.ts:151-165`) and AI4DEV-24 ruled on that boundary twice. To be
 135: unambiguous about which conflation is forbidden:
 136: 
 137: - **`h` is not re-parameterized. It stays concrete `AtHarness`.** Re-labelling the harness with the
 138:   suite's `W`/`Channel` is the door AI4DEV-24's Gate 1 and Gate 2 shut, and this item **must not
 139:   reopen it**. `OpenWorld.h` keeps its type and its comment.
 140: - `w` and `sut` are parameters of `OpenWorld` only, and they are now derived, not supplied.
 141: 
 142: For REQ-016 this makes `WorldOf<'req-016'>` resolve to the concrete `NotificationFixtureWorld`
 143: class rather than the `World` interface. That is *more* honest â€” it is what the producer really
 144: returns â€” and test bodies typed against `World` continue to work because the class implements it.
 145: 
 146: **Decision D3. The registry must be constrained so a world that cannot be torn down is rejected at
 147: the declaration**, i.e. the mapped type requires every entry's world to extend `WorldLike`. An
 148: adapter whose world forgets `teardown()` then fails in `suite-adapters.ts` where the entry is
 149: written, not in whichever suite first opens it.
 150: 
 151: **Decision D4. Close the last string-level gap with a runtime cross-check.**
 152: 
 153: Two strings must denote the same suite: the key used for the *type* lookup (`'req-016'`) and the
 154: path used for the *runtime* adapter load (`adapterUrl()` in `index.ts:32`, fed from the parsed AT
 155: id at `registry.ts:534`). They are currently independent.
 156: 
 157: `atTest` must therefore assert, at registration, that the AT id's requirement equals the bound
 158: requirement, and throw where it is written â€” the same doctrine the file already applies to a
 159: malformed id (`registry.ts:15`, `:44-49`). Once that holds, the type looked up and the module
 160: loaded are the same suite by construction, not by convention.
 161: 
 162: The message must name both values and the file, e.g.:
 163: `AT-017.03 was registered through a suite bound to req-016 â€” the harness would load req-016's
 164: fixture adapter while the type-check described req-017's`.
 165: 
 166: **Decision D5. Extend the alias doctrine to the suite contract types that now flow through the seam.**
 167: 
 168: After D1, `OpenWorld.sut` resolves to `NotificationsSut` and `OpenWorld.w` to the adapter's world â€”
 169: both declared in `tests/at/suites/req-016/_contract.ts` as **`interface`**, along with everything
 170: their methods return (`SenderProbe`, `RegisteredRow`, `DocumentedDefault`, `NotificationEvent`,
 171: `Delivery`, `OpsItem`, `EmitResult`, `World`).
 172: 
 173: AI4DEV-24's rule (`contracts.ts:151-165`, `type-invention.selftest.ts:50-84`) is that everything
 174: reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body must be a type alias, because an interface can
 175: be augmented and read green. These types are now exactly that. So they must become aliases and join
 176: the **exhaustive** `ALIAS_PROTECTED` list, which is explicitly a specification and not a sample.
 177: 
 178: I considered leaving this to a follow-up and **ruled against it**. Without it the item's own proof is
 179: nominal: it would close "a suite declares a different SUT type" and leave "a suite adds a member to
 180: the SUT type" open at the same seam, one line further down. Note the severity does drop either way â€”
 181: after D1 a *required* invented member breaks the producer with TS2741 â€” so what D5 actually kills is
 182: the *optional* member that reads `undefined`, which is precisely the case that slipped past two gates
 183: in AI4DEV-24 and is the reason the alias rule exists at all.
 184: 
 185: This is not scope drift: it is the same defect class, at the same seam, in the anchor the board item
 186: names. It is **not** H3/H4/H5 capability work and **not** a harness-factory redesign, both of which
 187: stay out.
 188: 
 189: ### Alternatives considered and rejected
 190: 
 191: | alternative | why rejected |
 192: |---|---|
 193: | Parameterize `AtHarness` / `createHarness` over the suite's `Sut` | This is the door AI4DEV-24 shut after two adversarial gates. Reopening it to close this hole would regress a fix that cost that item its Gate 1 and Gate 2. |
 194: | Runtime duck-type validation of the sut at `open()` (assert a method-name list) | The list cannot be derived from a type without a code generator, so it is a second hand-maintained declaration â€” the exact drift this item exists to remove. It would also make the proof a runtime test, where the brief requires a negative *type* test. |
 195: | Derive the requirement from the AT id at the type level with template-literal types | Requirement ids legitimately carry dots (`005.5` â€” `registry.ts:28-32`), so the parse is fragile, and it moves the derivation onto every call site instead of one place. |
 196: | Assert the shape at the consumer with a better-worded cast | It is still an assertion. The brief says prefer the checked producer, and the whole item is that assertions at this seam are the defect. |
 197: 
 198: ### The cost, stated plainly so it can be argued with
 199: 
 200: Every suite must have one line in `suite-adapters.ts`, and a new suite's `_bind.ts` will not compile
 201: until it is added. For thirty suites that is a thirty-line file. I judge that acceptable and in fact
 202: desirable â€” the harness's knowledge of which suites exist becomes explicit and checkable rather than
 203: a path convention â€” but it is a real ergonomic commitment beyond this item's own blast radius, so it
 204: is named here rather than buried. The compile error must therefore say exactly what to add.
