 208: ## 3. The proof â€” a negative test, demonstrated RED first
 209: 
 210: The established pattern is `tests/at/typeprobes/` (a program that MUST NOT compile) plus
 211: `tests/at/harness/type-invention.selftest.ts` (which runs the compiler over it and fails **by name**
 212: if any attack is accepted). This item extends it.
 213: 
 214: **A NEW probe file, `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam.probe.ts`**, kept separate from
 215: `harness-invention.probe.ts` so the red-then-green demonstration can compile it in isolation.
 216: 
 217: Every attack must be written in **the form a suite can use on today's tree**, so the same file is
 218: meaningful before and after the fix. That is what makes the demonstration honest.
 219: 
 220: | # | attack | must be rejected by |
 221: |---|---|---|
 222: | 5 | fabricate the SUT type: `bindSuite<{ notThere(): Promise<void> }, World>({ sut: 'notifications' })`, then call `sut.notThere()` in a body | TS2558 (arity) |
 223: | 6 | fabricate the world type the same way and read a member off `w` | TS2558 (arity) |
 224: | 7 | name a sut key the adapter does not supply (`sut: 'notificatoins'`) | assignability error on the key |
 225: | 8 | read a member off `opened.sut` that the adapter's sut does not declare | TS2339 |
 226: | 9 | read a member off `opened.w` that the adapter's world does not supply | TS2339 |
 227: | 10 | merge a member into `NotificationsSut` via `declare module` | TS2300 / TS6200 |
 228: | 11 | merge a member into `World` via `declare module` | TS2300 / TS6200 |
